{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795608695,"nonce":"GBOj0YjLZv1P4qp7DEGmwyypiZs=","title":"A rounded clip loses its corners inside a rounder one","message":"A panel with rounded corners and overflow clip, placed away from the corners of a frame that also clips and has a larger radius, draws with square corners.\n\nCause: `GpuPaintContext::get_clip_data_with_path_scissor` kept, per corner, only the clip with the largest radius. It then dropped the rounding when that clip's own corner was not near the intersection's corner. The frame won by radius and its corner was elsewhere, so the panel's corner became square. A radius between the two cut the corner on the wrong curve instead.\n\nFound on the heading select's list inside a rounded, clipped rich text editor (26002b452b75be9fe98ed2910cd304adffc0646930cfabb9a4f22c9e719c598b).\n\nFix: judge every rounded clip at the intersection's corner and take the largest radius that reaches it. Test: `nested_clips` in the recorder fails on the old code and passes on the new.","files":null}]}